<exercises xml:id="exercises-for-while-loops-extra">
	<title>💻 Extra Coding Exercises</title>

	<introduction>
		<p>
			These are supplemental advanced exercises that go beyond the core material. They are intended for students seeking additional challenge or extra practice with loops and flow control.
		</p>
	</introduction>

	<exercise label="hw-while-egyptian-fractions">
		<title>Egyptian Fraction Decomposition</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				An <term>unit fraction</term> is a fraction with numerator 1. The ancient Egyptians wrote every positive fraction as a sum of distinct unit fractions. For example,
				<me>
					\frac{5}{7} = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{5} + \frac{1}{70}.
				</me>
			</p>

			<p>
				A standard greedy algorithm builds this decomposition one term at a time. If the current fraction is <m>\frac{p}{q}</m>, choose
				<me>
					n = \left\lceil \frac{q}{p} \right\rceil,
				</me>
				so that <m>\frac{1}{n}</m> is the largest unit fraction that does not exceed <m>\frac{p}{q}</m>. Then subtract it and continue with the remainder.
			</p>

			<p>
				Write a function <pf>egyptian(p, q)</pf> that takes positive integers <pf>p</pf> and <pf>q</pf> with <m>0 &lt; p &lt; q</m> and prints the Egyptian fraction decomposition of <m>\frac{p}{q}</m>. Use a <pf>while</pf>-loop that continues while <pf>p &gt; 0</pf>. In each iteration:
			</p>

			<ol>
				<li>Compute <pf>n = ceil(q / p)</pf>.</li>
				<li>Print the next term <pf>1/n</pf>.</li>
				<li>Update the fraction using
					<me>
						\frac{p}{q} - \frac{1}{n} = \frac{pn - q}{qn}.
					</me>
					So set <pf>p = p*n - q</pf> and <pf>q = q*n</pf>.
				</li>
			</ol>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left" />
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>p</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>positive integer numerator</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>q</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>positive integer denominator, with <m>p &lt; q</m></p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p>none</p></cell>
					<cell><p>printed text</p></cell>
					<cell><p>the Egyptian fraction decomposition of <m>\frac{p}{q}</m></p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						egyptian(5, 7)
						egyptian(3, 11)
						egyptian(7, 15)
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						5/7 = 1/2 + 1/5 + 1/70
						3/11 = 1/4 + 1/44
						7/15 = 1/3 + 1/8 + 1/120
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function egyptian(p, q)
					origP = p;
					origQ = q;
					first = true;

					fprintf('%i/%i = ', origP, origQ)
					while p &gt; 0
						n = ceil(q / p);
						if ~first
							fprintf(' + ')
						end
						fprintf('1/%i', n)

						p = p * n - q;
						q = q * n;
						g = gcd(p, q);
						if g &gt; 0
							p = p / g;
							q = q / g;
						end

						first = false;
					end
					fprintf('\n')
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-while-archimedes-pi">
		<title>Archimedes' Method for <m>\pi</m></title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Archimedes approximated <m>\pi</m> by using regular polygons inscribed in a unit circle. If a regular <m>n</m>-gon is inscribed in a circle of radius 1, then its perimeter is
				<me>
					P_n = 2n\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right).
				</me>
				As <m>n</m> increases, the perimeter approaches the circle's circumference <m>2\pi</m>. Therefore,
				<me>
					\frac{P_n}{2} = n\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right)
				</me>
				approaches <m>\pi</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				Write a function <pf>archimedesPi(tol)</pf> that starts with <pf>n = 6</pf> and repeatedly doubles <pf>n</pf> in a <pf>while</pf>-loop. In each iteration, compute
				<me>
					\text{approx} = n\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{n}\right)
				</me>
				and continue until <pf>abs(approx - pi) &lt;= tol</pf>. Display the step number, the current value of <pf>n</pf>, the approximation, and the error at each step. Return the final <pf>n</pf>, the final approximation, and the number of doublings used.
			</p>

			<p>
				Because the error is proportional to <m>1/n^2</m>, doubling <m>n</m> reduces the error by about a factor of 4. So each doubling gives about <m>\log_{10}(4) \approx 0.60</m> additional correct decimal digits. This is much slower than Newton's method, which typically doubles the number of correct digits each iteration once it is close to the root.
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left" />
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>tol</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>stopping tolerance for <pf>abs(approx - pi)</pf></p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>n</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>final number of polygon sides</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>approx</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>final approximation of <m>\pi</m></p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>steps</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>number of times <pf>n</pf> was doubled</p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						[n, approx, steps] = archimedesPi(1e-2)
						[n, approx, steps] = archimedesPi(1e-6)
						[n, approx, steps] = archimedesPi(1e-10)
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						n = 24      approx = 3.132628613281238   steps = 2
						n = 3072    approx = 3.141592105999271   steps = 9
						n = 393216  approx = 3.141592653556370   steps = 16
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function [n, approx, steps] = archimedesPi(tol)
					n = 6;
					steps = 0;
					approx = n * sin(pi / n);
					err = abs(approx - pi);

					while err &gt; tol
						fprintf('Step %2i: n = %8i, approx = %.15f, error = %.3e\n', ...
							steps, n, approx, err)
						n = 2 * n;
						steps = steps + 1;
						approx = n * sin(pi / n);
						err = abs(approx - pi);
					end

					fprintf('Step %2i: n = %8i, approx = %.15f, error = %.3e\n', ...
						steps, n, approx, err)
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-collatz-conjecture-c">
		<title>Collatz Siblings</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Write a function <pf>collatzSiblings(x, a, b)</pf> that uses <pf>collatzSteps</pf> to count how many <em>other</em> integers in the interval from <pf>a</pf> through <pf>b</pf>, inclusive, have the same Collatz step count as <pf>x</pf>.
			</p>

			<p>
				For example, <pf>collatzSteps(6) = 8</pf>, but no other integer from 1 through 10 takes 8 steps, so <pf>collatzSiblings(6, 1, 10)</pf> should return <pf>0</pf>.
			</p>

			<p>
				Assume <pf>x</pf>, <pf>a</pf>, and <pf>b</pf> are positive integers with <pf>a &lt;= x &lt;= b</pf>.
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left" />
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>x</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>reference integer whose Collatz steps are being matched</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>a</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>starting integer in the interval</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>b</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>ending integer in the interval</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>count</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>number of integers in <m>[a,b]</m>, excluding <pf>x</pf>, with the same Collatz step count as <pf>x</pf></p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						count = collatzSiblings(6, 1, 10)
						count = collatzSiblings(10, 1, 100)
						count = collatzSiblings(27, 1, 1000)
						count = collatzSiblings(6171, 1, 10000)
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						count = 0
						count = 1
						count = 5
						count = 0
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function count = collatzSiblings(x, a, b)
					targetSteps = collatzSteps(x);
					count = 0;

					for n = a:b
						if n ~= x &amp;&amp; collatzSteps(n) == targetSteps
							count = count + 1;
						end
					end
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-fibonacci-golden-ratio-a">
		<title>Fibonacci Report</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Write a function <pf>fibonacciReport()</pf> that uses a <pf>while</pf>-loop to generate Fibonacci numbers until the first one exceeds <m>10000</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				As the function runs, display each Fibonacci number. Return the first Fibonacci number greater than <m>10000</m> in the variable <pf>fib_result</pf>.
			</p>

			<p>
				Use the Fibonacci sequence
				<me>
					0,\ 1,\ 1,\ 2,\ 3,\ 5,\ 8,\ 13,\ 21,\ \ldots
				</me>
				where each term is the sum of the previous two terms.
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left" />
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p>none</p></cell>
					<cell><p>—</p></cell>
					<cell><p>start the sequence with <m>0</m> and <m>1</m></p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>fib_result</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>first Fibonacci number greater than <m>10000</m></p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						fib_result = fibonacciReport()
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% display includes the Fibonacci numbers from 0 through 10946
						fib_result = 10946
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function fib_result = fibonacciReport()
					prev2 = 0;
					prev1 = 1;

					fprintf('%i\n', prev2)
					fprintf('%i\n', prev1)

					while prev1 &lt;= 10000
						current = prev1 + prev2;
						fprintf('%i\n', current)
						prev2 = prev1;
						prev1 = current;
					end

					fib_result = prev1;
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-fibonacci-golden-ratio-b">
		<title>Fibonacci Ratios</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Write a function <pf>fibonacciRatio()</pf> that uses a <pf>while</pf>-loop to generate Fibonacci numbers until the first one exceeds <m>10000</m>. At each step, compute the ratio of consecutive Fibonacci numbers,
				<me>
					\frac{F_n}{F_{n-1}}.
				</me>
			</p>

			<p>
				Return the final ratio in the variable <pf>ratio</pf>. This value should be close to the golden ratio
				<me>
					\varphi = \frac{1 + \sqrt{5}}{2} \approx 1.6180339887.
				</me>
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left" />
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p>none</p></cell>
					<cell><p>—</p></cell>
					<cell><p>start the sequence with <m>0</m> and <m>1</m></p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>ratio</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>ratio of the final two consecutive Fibonacci numbers generated</p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						ratio = fibonacciRatio()
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						ratio = 1.61803399852
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function ratio = fibonacciRatio()
					prev2 = 0;
					prev1 = 1;
					ratio = prev1;

					while prev1 &lt;= 10000
						current = prev1 + prev2;
						ratio = current / prev1;
						prev2 = prev1;
						prev1 = current;
					end
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-while-base-conversion">
		<title>Decimal to Binary Conversion</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				To convert a positive integer from decimal to binary, repeatedly divide by 2 and record the remainders. Reading those remainders from bottom to top gives the binary representation.
			</p>

			<p>
				For example,
				<md>
					<mrow>13 \div 2 = 6 \text{ remainder } 1</mrow>
					<mrow>6 \div 2 = 3 \text{ remainder } 0</mrow>
					<mrow>3 \div 2 = 1 \text{ remainder } 1</mrow>
					<mrow>1 \div 2 = 0 \text{ remainder } 1</mrow>
				</md>
				so <m>13_{10} = 1101_2</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				Write a function called <pf>toBinary</pf> that takes a positive integer <pf>n</pf> and returns its binary representation as an ordinary base-10 integer whose digits are the binary digits. For example, <pf>toBinary(13)</pf> should return <pf>1101</pf>.
			</p>

			<p>
				Use a variable named <pf>place</pf> that starts at 1 and is multiplied by 10 each iteration. If <pf>remainder = mod(n,2)</pf>, then update the result with <pf>result = result + remainder*place</pf>.
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>n</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>positive integer to convert</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>result</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>base-10 integer whose digits are the binary digits of <pf>n</pf></p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						toBinary(1)
						% Test 2
						toBinary(10)
						% Test 3
						toBinary(13)
						% Test 4
						toBinary(255)
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						ans = 1
						% Test 2
						ans = 1010
						% Test 3
						ans = 1101
						% Test 4
						ans = 11111111
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function result = toBinary(n)
					result = 0;
					place = 1;

					while n &gt; 0
						remainder = mod(n, 2);
						result = result + remainder * place;
						n = floor(n / 2);
						place = place * 10;
					end
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-while-digital-root-a">
		<title>Last Digit</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Write a function called <pf>lastDigit</pf> that returns the rightmost digit of a positive integer <pf>n</pf>. For example, <pf>lastDigit(123)</pf> should return <pf>3</pf>.
			</p>

			<p>
				Use the <pf>mod</pf> function to extract the ones digit.
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>n</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>positive integer</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>digit</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>rightmost digit of <pf>n</pf></p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						lastDigit(123)
						% Test 2
						lastDigit(90)
						% Test 3
						lastDigit(7)
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						ans = 3
						% Test 2
						ans = 0
						% Test 3
						ans = 7
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function digit = lastDigit(n)
					digit = mod(n, 10);
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-while-digital-root-b">
		<title>Remove Last Digit</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Write a function called <pf>removeLastDigit</pf> that removes the rightmost digit of a positive integer <pf>n</pf>. For example, <pf>removeLastDigit(123)</pf> should return <pf>12</pf>.
			</p>

			<p>
				You can remove the last digit by dividing by 10 and rounding down.
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>n</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>positive integer</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>remaining</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>value of <pf>n</pf> with its rightmost digit removed</p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						removeLastDigit(123)
						% Test 2
						removeLastDigit(90)
						% Test 3
						removeLastDigit(7)
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						ans = 12
						% Test 2
						ans = 9
						% Test 3
						ans = 0
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function remaining = removeLastDigit(n)
					remaining = floor(n / 10);
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-while-digital-root-c">
		<title>Digital Root with Helper Functions</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				The digital root of a positive integer is found by repeatedly summing its digits until only one digit remains. For example,
				<md>
					<mrow>9 + 8 + 7 + 5 = 29</mrow>
					<mrow>2 + 9 = 11</mrow>
					<mrow>1 + 1 = 2</mrow>
				</md>
				so the digital root of <m>9875</m> is <m>2</m>.
			</p>

			<p>
				Write a function called <pf>digitalRoot</pf> that returns the digital root of a positive integer <pf>n</pf>. Use <pf>lastDigit</pf> and <pf>removeLastDigit</pf> as helper functions, and use <pf>while</pf>-loops to process the digits.
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>n</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>positive integer</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>root</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>digital root of <pf>n</pf></p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						digitalRoot(38)
						% Test 2
						digitalRoot(99)
						% Test 3
						digitalRoot(123456)
						% Test 4
						digitalRoot(999999)
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						ans = 2
						% Test 2
						ans = 9
						% Test 3
						ans = 3
						% Test 4
						ans = 9
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function root = digitalRoot(n)
					while n &gt;= 10
						temp = n;
						sum_digits = 0;

						while temp &gt; 0
							sum_digits = sum_digits + lastDigit(temp);
							temp = removeLastDigit(temp);
						end

						n = sum_digits;
					end

					root = n;
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-base-conversion-a">
		<title>Build a Number from Random Digits</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Write a function called <pf>buildRandomNumber</pf> that constructs a number from <pf>n</pf> randomly generated digits. Use a <pf>while</pf>-loop and generate each digit with <pf>randi([0,9])</pf>.
			</p>

			<p>
				Treat the first generated digit as the ones digit, the second as the tens digit, the third as the hundreds digit, and so on. For example, if <pf>n = 4</pf> and the generated digits are <m>7</m>, <m>3</m>, <m>0</m>, and <m>5</m>, then the final number is
				<me>x = 7\cdot 10^0 + 3\cdot 10^1 + 0\cdot 10^2 + 5\cdot 10^3 = 5037.</me>
			</p>

			<p>
				Print each step as the number is built. Use <pf>x</pf> for the current value, <pf>x0</pf> for the previous value, <pf>k</pf> for the place value index, and <pf>digit</pf> for the random digit generated at each step.
			</p>

			<p>
				Because the digits are random, your exact result will vary from one run to the next. Also note that if the last digit generated is 0, the returned number may display with fewer than <pf>n</pf> digits.
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>n</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>number of random digits to generate</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>x</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>constructed number</p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Example Calls ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Example 1
						x = buildRandomNumber(1)
						% Example 2
						x = buildRandomNumber(4)
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>What to Expect ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Example 1
						% x is an integer from 0 to 9.
						% One line of output is printed.
						
						% Example 2
						% x is an integer from 0 to 9999.
						% Four lines of intermediate output are printed,
						% followed by the final number.
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function x = buildRandomNumber(n)
					x = 0;
					x0 = 0;
					k = 0;

					while k &lt; n
						digit = randi([0, 9]);
						fprintf('k = %i, digit = %i: ', k, digit);
						x = x + digit * 10^k;
						fprintf('x = %i + %i*10^%i = %i\n', x0, digit, k, x);
						x0 = x;
						k = k + 1;
					end

					fprintf('Final number: %i\n', x)
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-base-conversion-b">
		<title>Print Digits from Right to Left</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Write a function called <pf>printDigits</pf> that prints the digits of a positive integer one at a time, starting with the ones digit and moving left.
			</p>

			<p>
				Use the following two-step process inside a <pf>while</pf>-loop:
				<ul>
					<li>Extract the rightmost digit with <pf>mod(n,10)</pf>.</li>
					<li>Remove that digit with <pf>floor(n/10)</pf>.</li>
				</ul>
			</p>

			<p>
				For example, <pf>printDigits(1234)</pf> should print <pf>4321</pf>. The function does not return a value; it prints directly to the Command Window.
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>n</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>positive integer</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p>none</p></cell>
					<cell><p>—</p></cell>
					<cell><p>prints the digits of <pf>n</pf> from right to left</p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						printDigits(1234)
						% Test 2
						printDigits(50374254)
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1 prints
						4321
						% Test 2 prints
						45247305
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function printDigits(n)
					while n &gt; 0
						digit = mod(n, 10);
						fprintf('%i', digit);
						n = floor(n / 10);
					end
					fprintf('\n');
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-random-walk-a">
		<title>Random Walk to a Boundary</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				A <term>random walk</term> changes position by a sequence of steps. In a live simulation, each step could be generated with <pf>randi([0,1]) * 2 - 1</pf>, which produces either <m>-1</m> or <m>1</m> with equal probability.
			</p>

			<p>
				To make the exercise testable, write a function called <pf>walkToBoundary</pf> that takes a starting position, a lower boundary, an upper boundary, and a vector of moves whose entries are <pf>-1</pf> or <pf>1</pf>. Use a <pf>while</pf>-loop to apply moves one at a time until the position reaches either boundary or there are no moves left.
			</p>

			<p>
				Return the final position, the number of moves actually used, and the highest and lowest positions reached. This models both a walk that starts at 0 and stops at <m>-10</m> or <m>10</m>, and the Gambler's Ruin version that starts at 5 and stops at 0 or 10.
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>startPos</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>starting position</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>lowerBound</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>lower stopping value</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>upperBound</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>upper stopping value</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>moves</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1xn) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>vector of steps, each equal to <pf>-1</pf> or <pf>1</pf></p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>finalPos</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>position where the walk stops</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>steps</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>number of moves used</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>maxPos</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>highest position reached</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>minPos</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>lowest position reached</p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						[finalPos, n, hi, lo] = walkToBoundary(0, -3, 3, [1 1 -1 1 1])
						% Test 2
						[finalPos, n, hi, lo] = walkToBoundary(5, 0, 10, [-1 -1 1 -1 -1 -1 -1])
						% Test 3
						[finalPos, n, hi, lo] = walkToBoundary(5, 0, 10, [1 1 -1 1 1 1 1])
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						finalPos = 3		n = 5		hi = 3		lo = 0
						% Test 2
						finalPos = 0		n = 7		hi = 5		lo = 0
						% Test 3
						finalPos = 10		n = 7		hi = 10		lo = 5
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function [finalPos, steps, maxPos, minPos] = walkToBoundary(startPos, lowerBound, upperBound, moves)
					pos = startPos;
					steps = 0;
					maxPos = pos;
					minPos = pos;
					idx = 1;

					while pos &gt; lowerBound &amp;&amp; pos &lt; upperBound &amp;&amp; idx &lt;= length(moves)
						pos = pos + moves(idx);
						steps = steps + 1;

						if pos &gt; maxPos
							maxPos = pos;
						end

						if pos &lt; minPos
							minPos = pos;
						end

						idx = idx + 1;
					end

					finalPos = pos;
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-guessing-game-a">
		<title>Number Guessing Game</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				The original version of this activity uses <pf>randi</pf> to choose a secret number and <pf>input</pf> to collect guesses from the player. To make the behavior testable, write a function called <pf>guessNumber</pf> that takes the secret number and a vector of guesses as input.
			</p>

			<p>
				Process the guesses in order with a <pf>while</pf>-loop until the correct guess is found or the list of guesses runs out. Return <pf>won</pf> as 1 if the player guessed correctly and 0 otherwise, along with <pf>attempts</pf>, the number of guesses that were actually used.
			</p>

			<p>
				In an interactive version, you would print <q>Too low!</q> or <q>Too high!</q> after each incorrect guess.
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>secret</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>secret number to guess</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>guesses</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1xn) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>guesses to process in order</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>won</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>1 if the secret number is guessed, otherwise 0</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>attempts</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>number of guesses used</p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						[won, n] = guessNumber(42, [50 25 40 42])
						% Test 2
						[won, n] = guessNumber(17, [10 20 17])
						% Test 3
						[won, n] = guessNumber(8, [1 2 3])
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						won = 1			n = 4
						% Test 2
						won = 1			n = 3
						% Test 3
						won = 0			n = 3
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function [won, attempts] = guessNumber(secret, guesses)
					won = 0;
					attempts = 0;
					idx = 1;

					while idx &lt;= length(guesses) &amp;&amp; won == 0
						attempts = attempts + 1;

						if guesses(idx) == secret
							won = 1;
						end

						idx = idx + 1;
					end
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-guessing-game-c">
		<title>Limited Guesses</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Write a function called <pf>guessNumberLimited</pf> that works like the previous exercise, but stops after at most <pf>maxAttempts</pf> guesses. Process the guesses in order with a <pf>while</pf>-loop.
			</p>

			<p>
				Return <pf>won</pf> as 1 if the player guesses the secret number before the limit is reached and 0 otherwise. Also return <pf>attempts</pf>, the number of guesses actually used.
			</p>

			<p>
				In the interactive version, if the player loses, you would reveal the secret number after the loop ends.
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>secret</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>secret number to guess</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>guesses</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1xn) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>guesses to process in order</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>maxAttempts</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>maximum number of guesses allowed</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>won</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>1 if the player guesses correctly in time, otherwise 0</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>attempts</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>number of guesses used before stopping</p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						[won, n] = guessNumberLimited(42, [50 25 40 42], 4)
						% Test 2
						[won, n] = guessNumberLimited(42, [50 25 40 35 44], 3)
						% Test 3
						[won, n] = guessNumberLimited(7, [7 1 2], 7)
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						won = 1			n = 4
						% Test 2
						won = 0			n = 3
						% Test 3
						won = 1			n = 1
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function [won, attempts] = guessNumberLimited(secret, guesses, maxAttempts)
					won = 0;
					attempts = 0;
					idx = 1;

					while idx &lt;= length(guesses) &amp;&amp; attempts &lt; maxAttempts &amp;&amp; won == 0
						attempts = attempts + 1;

						if guesses(idx) == secret
							won = 1;
						end

						idx = idx + 1;
					end
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-mental-math-a">
		<title>Multiplication Streak</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				The original version of this activity generates random multiplication questions and stops as soon as the player gives one incorrect answer. To make the logic testable, write a function called <pf>multiplicationStreak</pf> that takes three equally sized vectors: the first factors, the second factors, and the player's answers.
			</p>

			<p>
				Use a <pf>while</pf>-loop to process the questions in order. Stop at the first incorrect answer or when all questions have been checked. Return the number of consecutive correct answers from the start.
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>aVals</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1xn) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>first factors</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>bVals</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1xn) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>second factors</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>answers</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1xn) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>player answers</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>streak</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>number of consecutive correct answers from the start</p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						multiplicationStreak([3 7 2], [4 5 9], [12 35 18])
						% Test 2
						multiplicationStreak([3 7 2], [4 5 9], [12 34 18])
						% Test 3
						multiplicationStreak([9 8], [9 7], [80 56])
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						ans = 3
						% Test 2
						ans = 1
						% Test 3
						ans = 0
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function streak = multiplicationStreak(aVals, bVals, answers)
					streak = 0;
					idx = 1;

					while idx &lt;= length(answers) &amp;&amp; answers(idx) == aVals(idx) * bVals(idx)
						streak = streak + 1;
						idx = idx + 1;
					end
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-mental-math-b">
		<title>Mixed Operations Streak</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Write a function called <pf>mixedMathStreak</pf> that checks a sequence of arithmetic questions. Use operation codes <pf>1</pf> for addition, <pf>2</pf> for subtraction, and <pf>3</pf> for multiplication.
			</p>

			<p>
				The inputs <pf>ops</pf>, <pf>aVals</pf>, <pf>bVals</pf>, and <pf>answers</pf> should all have the same length. Use a <pf>while</pf>-loop and a <pf>switch</pf>-statement to compute the correct answer for each round. Stop at the first incorrect answer and return the number of consecutive correct answers from the start.
			</p>

			<p>
				Assume every subtraction round is written so that <pf>aVals(k) &gt;= bVals(k)</pf>.
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>ops</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1xn) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>operation codes: 1 add, 2 subtract, 3 multiply</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>aVals</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1xn) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>first numbers</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>bVals</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1xn) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>second numbers</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>answers</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1xn) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>player answers</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>streak</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>number of consecutive correct answers from the start</p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						mixedMathStreak([1 2 3 1], [12 9 7 40], [5 4 6 2], [17 5 42 42])
						% Test 2
						mixedMathStreak([1 2 3 1], [12 9 7 40], [5 4 6 2], [17 5 41 42])
						% Test 3
						mixedMathStreak([2], [10], [3], [8])
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						ans = 4
						% Test 2
						ans = 2
						% Test 3
						ans = 0
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function streak = mixedMathStreak(ops, aVals, bVals, answers)
					streak = 0;
					idx = 1;

					while idx &lt;= length(answers)
						switch ops(idx)
							case 1
								trueAnswer = aVals(idx) + bVals(idx);
							case 2
								trueAnswer = aVals(idx) - bVals(idx);
							case 3
								trueAnswer = aVals(idx) * bVals(idx);
						end

						if answers(idx) == trueAnswer
							streak = streak + 1;
							idx = idx + 1;
						else
							break
						end
					end
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-rps-a">
		<title>Rock, Paper, Scissors Round</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				In Rock, Paper, Scissors, the choices are encoded as <pf>1 = Rock</pf>, <pf>2 = Paper</pf>, and <pf>3 = Scissors</pf>. A player wins if Rock beats Scissors, Paper beats Rock, or Scissors beats Paper.
			</p>

			<p>
				To make the activity testable, write a function called <pf>rpsRound</pf> that takes the player's choice and the computer's choice as input and returns the result of the round.
			</p>

			<p>
				Return <pf>winner</pf> as <pf>1</pf> if the player wins, <pf>0</pf> if the round is a tie, and <pf>-1</pf> if the computer wins. In an interactive version, the computer's move could be generated with <pf>randi([1,3])</pf>.
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>player</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>player's choice: <pf>1</pf> for Rock, <pf>2</pf> for Paper, <pf>3</pf> for Scissors</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>comp</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>computer's choice: <pf>1</pf> for Rock, <pf>2</pf> for Paper, <pf>3</pf> for Scissors</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>winner</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>1</pf> if the player wins, <pf>0</pf> for a tie, and <pf>-1</pf> if the computer wins</p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						rpsRound(1, 3)
						% Test 2
						rpsRound(2, 2)
						% Test 3
						rpsRound(3, 1)
						% Test 4
						rpsRound(2, 1)
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						ans = 1
						% Test 2
						ans = 0
						% Test 3
						ans = -1
						% Test 4
						ans = 1
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function winner = rpsRound(player, comp)
					if player == comp
						winner = 0;
					elseif (player == 1 &amp;&amp; comp == 3) || ...
								(player == 2 &amp;&amp; comp == 1) || ...
								(player == 3 &amp;&amp; comp == 2)
						winner = 1;
					else
						winner = -1;
					end
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-rps-b">
		<title>Rock, Paper, Scissors First to 3 Wins</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Write a function called <pf>rpsFirstToThree</pf> that plays a match using predetermined moves for the player and the computer. This makes the activity testable without <pf>input</pf> or random numbers.
			</p>

			<p>
				The vectors <pf>playerMoves</pf> and <pf>compMoves</pf> contain choices encoded as <pf>1 = Rock</pf>, <pf>2 = Paper</pf>, and <pf>3 = Scissors</pf>. Use a <pf>while</pf>-loop to process moves in order until either the player reaches 3 wins, the computer reaches 3 wins, or one of the move lists runs out.
			</p>

			<p>
				Return the final scores, the number of rounds actually played, and <pf>winner</pf>, where <pf>1</pf> means the player won the match, <pf>-1</pf> means the computer won the match, and <pf>0</pf> means no one reached 3 wins before the moves ended. Ties count as rounds played but do not increase either score.
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>playerMoves</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1xn) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>player choices to process in order</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>compMoves</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1xm) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>computer choices to process in order</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>playerScore</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>number of rounds won by the player</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>compScore</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>number of rounds won by the computer</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>rounds</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>number of rounds actually played</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>winner</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>1</pf> if the player wins the match, <pf>-1</pf> if the computer wins, and <pf>0</pf> otherwise</p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						[p, c, n, w] = rpsFirstToThree([1 2 3 1], [3 1 2 2])
						% Test 2
						[p, c, n, w] = rpsFirstToThree([1 1 1 2 2], [2 2 1 3 3])
						% Test 3
						[p, c, n, w] = rpsFirstToThree([1 2 3], [1 3 2])
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						p = 3		c = 0		n = 3		w = 1
						% Test 2
						p = 0		c = 3		n = 4		w = -1
						% Test 3
						p = 1		c = 1		n = 3		w = 0
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function [playerScore, compScore, rounds, winner] = rpsFirstToThree(playerMoves, compMoves)
					playerScore = 0;
					compScore = 0;
					rounds = 0;
					idx = 1;
					maxRounds = min(length(playerMoves), length(compMoves));

					while playerScore &lt; 3 &amp;&amp; compScore &lt; 3 &amp;&amp; idx &lt;= maxRounds
						player = playerMoves(idx);
						comp = compMoves(idx);
						rounds = rounds + 1;

						if player ~= comp
							if (player == 1 &amp;&amp; comp == 3) || ...
								(player == 2 &amp;&amp; comp == 1) || ...
								(player == 3 &amp;&amp; comp == 2)
								playerScore = playerScore + 1;
							else
								compScore = compScore + 1;
							end
						end

						idx = idx + 1;
					end

					if playerScore == 3
						winner = 1;
					elseif compScore == 3
						winner = -1;
					else
						winner = 0;
					end
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-rps-c">
		<title>Rock, Paper, Scissors Match Statistics</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Write a function called <pf>rpsMatchStats</pf> that tracks the full summary of a Rock, Paper, Scissors match. Use the same move encoding as before: <pf>1 = Rock</pf>, <pf>2 = Paper</pf>, and <pf>3 = Scissors</pf>.
			</p>

			<p>
				Use a <pf>while</pf>-loop to process the moves in order until either player reaches 3 wins or one of the move lists runs out. Track the total number of rounds played, the player's wins, the computer's wins, and the number of ties.
			</p>

			<p>
				Return <pf>winner</pf> as <pf>1</pf> if the player wins the match, <pf>-1</pf> if the computer wins, and <pf>0</pf> if no one reaches 3 wins.
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>playerMoves</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1xn) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>player choices to process in order</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>compMoves</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1xm) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>computer choices to process in order</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>playerWins</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>number of rounds won by the player</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>compWins</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>number of rounds won by the computer</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>ties</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>number of tied rounds</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>rounds</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>number of rounds actually played</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>winner</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>1</pf> if the player wins the match, <pf>-1</pf> if the computer wins, and <pf>0</pf> otherwise</p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						[p, c, t, n, w] = rpsMatchStats([1 2 3 1], [1 1 1 3])
						% Test 2
						[p, c, t, n, w] = rpsMatchStats([1 2 3 1], [3 1 2 2])
						% Test 3
						[p, c, t, n, w] = rpsMatchStats([1 1 1 2 2], [2 2 1 3 3])
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						p = 2		c = 1		t = 1		n = 4		w = 0
						% Test 2
						p = 3		c = 0		t = 0		n = 3		w = 1
						% Test 3
						p = 0		c = 3		t = 1		n = 4		w = -1
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function [playerWins, compWins, ties, rounds, winner] = rpsMatchStats(playerMoves, compMoves)
					playerWins = 0;
					compWins = 0;
					ties = 0;
					rounds = 0;
					idx = 1;
					maxRounds = min(length(playerMoves), length(compMoves));

					while playerWins &lt; 3 &amp;&amp; compWins &lt; 3 &amp;&amp; idx &lt;= maxRounds
						player = playerMoves(idx);
						comp = compMoves(idx);
						rounds = rounds + 1;

						if player == comp
							ties = ties + 1;
						elseif (player == 1 &amp;&amp; comp == 3) || ...
									(player == 2 &amp;&amp; comp == 1) || ...
									(player == 3 &amp;&amp; comp == 2)
							playerWins = playerWins + 1;
						else
							compWins = compWins + 1;
						end

						idx = idx + 1;
					end

					if playerWins == 3
						winner = 1;
					elseif compWins == 3
						winner = -1;
					else
						winner = 0;
					end
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-change-machine-a">
		<title>Counting Quarters</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				A vending machine often makes change by repeatedly taking the largest coin that still fits. This strategy is called a <term>greedy algorithm</term>.
			</p>

			<p>
				Write a function called <pf>countQuarters</pf> that takes an amount in cents and uses a <pf>while</pf>-loop to count how many quarters fit into that amount. Do not use division or <pf>floor</pf>. Instead, subtract <pf>25</pf> repeatedly until fewer than 25 cents remain.
			</p>

			<p>
				Return the number of quarters used and the remaining number of cents.
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>amount</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>nonnegative integer amount in cents</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>quarters</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>number of quarters used</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>remainder</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>cents left after removing all possible quarters</p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						[q, r] = countQuarters(87)
						% Test 2
						[q, r] = countQuarters(19)
						% Test 3
						[q, r] = countQuarters(100)
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						q = 3			r = 12
						% Test 2
						q = 0			r = 19
						% Test 3
						q = 4			r = 0
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function [quarters, remainder] = countQuarters(amount)
					quarters = 0;
					remainder = amount;

					while remainder &gt;= 25
						remainder = remainder - 25;
						quarters = quarters + 1;
					end
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-password-a">
		<title>PIN Check with Repeated Guesses</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				In a live version of this activity, a program could use <pf>input</pf> to keep asking for a PIN until the correct value is entered.
			</p>

			<p>
				To make the behavior testable, write a function called <pf>checkPin</pf> that takes the secret PIN and a vector of guesses. Use a <pf>while</pf>-loop to process guesses in order until the correct PIN is found or the guess list runs out.
			</p>

			<p>
				Return <pf>granted</pf> as <pf>1</pf> if the PIN is entered correctly and <pf>0</pf> otherwise. Also return <pf>attempts</pf>, the number of guesses that were actually used.
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>pin</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>secret PIN value</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>guesses</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1xn) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>PIN guesses to process in order</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>granted</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>1</pf> if the correct PIN is found, otherwise <pf>0</pf></p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>attempts</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>number of guesses used</p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						[g, n] = checkPin(1234, [1111 9999 1234])
						% Test 2
						[g, n] = checkPin(2468, [1111 2222 3333 4444])
						% Test 3
						[g, n] = checkPin(4321, [4321])
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						g = 1			n = 3
						% Test 2
						g = 0			n = 4
						% Test 3
						g = 1			n = 1
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function [granted, attempts] = checkPin(pin, guesses)
					granted = 0;
					attempts = 0;
					idx = 1;

					while idx &lt;= length(guesses) &amp;&amp; granted == 0
						attempts = attempts + 1;

						if guesses(idx) == pin
							granted = 1;
						end

						idx = idx + 1;
					end
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

	<exercise label="hw-password-b">
		<title>PIN Lockout After Three Strikes</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				Write a function called <pf>checkPinThreeStrikes</pf> that limits the user to at most three guesses.
			</p>

			<p>
				Use a <pf>while</pf>-loop with a compound condition so the loop continues only while the correct PIN has not been entered and fewer than 3 attempts have been used. Process guesses in order from the input vector.
			</p>

			<p>
				Return <pf>granted</pf> as <pf>1</pf> if the correct PIN is entered and <pf>0</pf> otherwise. Also return <pf>attempts</pf>, the number of guesses that were actually used, and <pf>lockedOut</pf>, which should be <pf>1</pf> only when all three attempts are used without entering the correct PIN. In an interactive version, after the second failed guess you could display the warning <q>1 attempt remaining before lockout.</q>
			</p>

			<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
				<col width="11%" halign="right" />
				<col width="15%" halign="left" />
				<col width="28%" halign="center" />
				<col width="46%" halign="left"/>
				<row>
					<cell>Inputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>pin</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>secret PIN value</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>guesses</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1xn) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>PIN guesses to process in order</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell>Outputs:</cell>
					<cell><p><pf>granted</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>1</pf> if the correct PIN is entered, otherwise <pf>0</pf></p></cell>
				</row>
				<row>
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>attempts</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p>number of guesses used, up to a maximum of 3</p></cell>
				</row>
				<row bottom="minor">
					<cell></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>lockedOut</pf></p></cell>
					<cell><p>(1x1) double</p></cell>
					<cell><p><pf>1</pf> if three incorrect guesses are used, otherwise <pf>0</pf></p></cell>
				</row>
			</tabular>

			<sidebyside widths="48% 50%">
				<stack>
					<p>Test Cases ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						[g, n, locked] = checkPinThreeStrikes(1234, [1111 1234 9999])
						% Test 2
						[g, n, locked] = checkPinThreeStrikes(1234, [1111 2222 3333 1234])
						% Test 3
						[g, n, locked] = checkPinThreeStrikes(2468, [2468])
					</program>
				</stack>
				<stack>
					<p>Expected ⤵︎</p>
					<program language="matlab">
						% Test 1
						g = 1			n = 2		locked = 0
						% Test 2
						g = 0			n = 3		locked = 1
						% Test 3
						g = 1			n = 1		locked = 0
					</program>
				</stack>
			</sidebyside>
		</statement>
		<solution>
			<program language="matlab">
				function [granted, attempts, lockedOut] = checkPinThreeStrikes(pin, guesses)
					granted = 0;
					attempts = 0;
					idx = 1;

					while granted == 0 &amp;&amp; attempts &lt; 3 &amp;&amp; idx &lt;= length(guesses)
						attempts = attempts + 1;

						if guesses(idx) == pin
							granted = 1;
						end

						idx = idx + 1;
					end

					if granted == 0 &amp;&amp; attempts == 3
						lockedOut = 1;
					else
						lockedOut = 0;
					end
				end
			</program>
		</solution>
	</exercise>

</exercises>